-- title:
--   Intrinsic differentiation preserves the defining ideal of the group
-- statement:
--   For every analytic subgroup, translation point $g$, and order bound $T$, the intrinsically defined differential ideal of the ambient defining ideal of $G$ equals that defining ideal. The local sections are the actual normalized mixed derivatives, and local generation uses regular rational coefficients on Zariski neighborhoods. Every defining equation pulls back to zero, so every such jet section vanishes. Conversely every homogeneous equation vanishing on $G$ is locally generated by the empty sum. This is the intrinsic version of the preservation assertion following Definition 4.2; comparison with the polynomial presentation is a separate theorem.
-- source:
--   Philippon (1986), Lemmes de zéros dans les groupes algébriques commutatifs, §4.1, printed pp. 371–374; https://numdam.org/articles/10.24033/bsmf.2060/ .

import Definitions.Def_PhilipponMultiplicity_SectionFour
set_option autoImplicit false
open scoped BigOperators Topology
open PhilipponMultiplicity

theorem PhilipponMultiplicity.differentialIdeal_vanishingIdeal
    (K : Type*) [NontriviallyNormedField K]
    (G : EmbeddedGroupProduct K) (A : AnalyticSubgroup G) (g : G.Point) (T : ℕ) :
    differentialIdeal A g T (G.vanishingIdeal Set.univ) = G.vanishingIdeal Set.univ := by sorry
